+<section class="chapter" id="chord-follow" data-n="21" data-group="MAKE MUSIC" data-video="E7aYH2h27BE">
+  <div class="ch-head"><span class="num">21</span><div><p class="group">MAKE MUSIC</p><h2>Chord follow</h2></div></div>
+  <p class="meta"><b>You'll learn</b> to write a bass line or an arp once, in its own key, and let another track's chords move it:
+    FOLLOW, its three modes, which note of the chord counts, and chords played live.</p>
+  <div class="video"></div>
+  <ol class="steps">
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/as-written">
+      <h3>As written</h3>
+      <p><kbd>PLAY</kbd>. The beat from <a href="#first-beat">chapter 3</a> plays, with four chords on track 2, the pad, a bar each: A
+        minor, F, C and G. The bass on track 1 plays what was written, on A under all four. On your own unit, any chords on another
+        track will do: chapters <a href="#keys/chord">5</a> and <a href="#recording">7</a> play and record them.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/own-key">
+      <h3>Its own key</h3>
+      <p>The track that moves says what key it's written in. On track 1 hold <kbd>SEL</kbd> for its <a href="#g-layer">layer</a>, tap
+        <kbd>A3</kbd> for ROOT A, and turn <kbd>KNOB 2</kbd> to MIN. A line plays as written under the chord of its track's ROOT.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/follow">
+      <h3>Follow</h3>
+      <p>Tap <kbd>SEL</kbd> three times: SCALE, CHORD, then FOLLOW. <kbd>KNOB 1</kbd>, FOLLOW, is OFF or the track whose chords this one
+        follows, never itself. T2: the bass goes to F, C and G with the pad. Only this track is set; the chord track needs nothing.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/page">
+      <h3>The page</h3>
+      <p>The page shows the chord, its notes on a keyboard, the note it goes by lit, and what this track does with it: T2 KEY -4 is four
+        semitones down under F. KEY moves every note by the distance from the track's ROOT to that note, the nearest way: up to six
+        semitones up, five down.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/off-on">
+      <h3>Off and on</h3>
+      <p><kbd>KNOB 1</kbd> back to OFF: the bass plays as written. T2 again: it follows. The steps never change: what you record or enter
+        on a following track stays what you played, and moves with the chords when it plays back. STOP clears the chords too: the page
+        says T2: NO CHORD YET, and the track plays as written until its chord track plays again.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/scale">
+      <h3>Scale</h3>
+      <p>KEY keeps the line's shape, so notes can leave the key: under F the bass's C is A♭. <kbd>KNOB 2</kbd>, MODE SCALE, moves the notes
+        along the track's own SCALE by as many scale steps instead (T2 SCALE -2), and every note stays in A minor. On CHR, SCALE moves
+        as KEY does.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/note">
+      <h3>Which note</h3>
+      <p><kbd>KNOB 3</kbd>, NOTE, is which note of the chord counts, for KEY and SCALE: its ROOT, worked out from its notes, inversions too
+        (F for A C F); LOW, its lowest (A there); HIGH, its highest; or LAST, the one started last. Here HIGH, then LOW: the page lights
+        the note it goes by, and the bass walks A A G G.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/fit">
+      <h3>Fit</h3>
+      <p><kbd>ALGORITHM</kbd> to track 3: this page now shows its own FOLLOW, OFF. <kbd>KNOB 1</kbd>: T2. <kbd>KNOB 2</kbd>, MODE FIT: every
+        note moves to the nearest note of the chord itself (a tie goes down), so NOTE dims. Keys follow as steps do: whatever you play
+        lands on the chord.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/arp">
+      <h3>An arp</h3>
+      <p>Every note a following track sounds moves as it starts: its steps, its keys, MIDI IN and its ARP. <kbd>ARP</kbd>, <kbd>KNOB 1</kbd>
+        MODE UP, <kbd>SEL</kbd> back to FOLLOW, and hold three keys: the arp moves through the chords on its own. A note already sounding,
+        or tied on, keeps its pitch; on a step where the chord changes, the chord comes first.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/live">
+      <h3>Lead from the keys</h3>
+      <p>A track's chord is the notes of its latest step, or the keys and MIDI notes it holds: a key pressed with none held starts a new
+        chord, the ones pressed while it is held join it, and it stays until the next one. Hold <kbd>GLO</kbd> and tap <kbd>G♯3</kbd> to
+        mute the pad; a muted track would still lead, so on track 1 <kbd>KNOB 1</kbd> goes on to T5. <kbd>ALGORITHM</kbd> to track 5, the
+        piano set as in <a href="#keys">chapter 5</a>. <kbd>SEL</kbd>, <kbd>SELECT</kbd> back to CHORD, and <kbd>OCT−</kbd> for a lower
+        hand: each white key plays a chord of A minor, and the bass goes with it.</p>
+    </li>
+    <li class="step" id="chord-follow/tune">
+      <h3>A tune above it</h3>
+      <p>On QUANTIZE SOLO (<a href="#keys/solo">chapter 5</a>) the black keys are a tune, not the chord: the following tracks stay on the
+        white key's chord while your other hand plays. That holds for live keys only: recorded, a tune is steps like any others, and
+        every step with notes is the chord track's chord. So record a tune on a track of its own. Drum kits neither follow nor lead,
+        MIDI OUT sends the keys as pressed, and a Skiff before 2.1 ignores FOLLOW: the tracks play as written.</p>
+    </li>
+  </ol>
+</section>
+
